<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=logarithm">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Logarithm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1614">1614</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A logarithm is the exponent to which a fixed number, the base, must be raised to produce a given number; logarithms were introduced by John Napier in 1614, in his book Mirifici Logarithmorum Canonis Descriptio, as a means of simplifying calculations. With logarithm tables, multi-digit multiplication could be replaced by table look-ups and simpler addition, and navigators, scientists, engineers and surveyors rapidly adopted them. Henry Briggs's table of 1617 introduced 10 as the base, and the slide rule, also based on logarithms, remained an essential calculating tool until the 1970s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/logarithm.jpg" alt="Logarithm" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Napier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirifici_Logarithmorum_canonis_Descriptio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
